## How the two meet

The firmware and the host tooling are **Available Components** as CERN-OHL-S-2.0
§1.7(a) defines them: Apache-2.0 is a Compatible Licence under §1.2(c), so they
stay Apache-2.0 inside a product made from this hardware, and §3.3(d) excludes
them from the obligation to license a modified whole under CERN-OHL-S. The
declaration runs one way. Apache-2.0 code may be taken into a CERN-OHL-S design;
nothing in CERN-OHL-S reaches back onto the firmware.

The INA226AIDGSR and the STM32F411CEU6 are Available Components under §1.7(b)(i),
being purchasable parts with public datasheets. Neither license reaches inside
them.

**If you modify the hardware design**, CERN-OHL-S §3.3 asks three things of you:
retain the notices, add one saying what you changed and when, and publish your
modified design under CERN-OHL-S with your own Source Location. Conveying a
board, modified or not, carries the §4 obligation to give each recipient the
complete design source or the location of it.

**The variant letter matters.** CERN-OHL-2.0 is three separate licenses. This
hardware is the **S** variant, strongly reciprocal. A reference to
"CERN-OHL-2.0" without the letter states nothing.

**The version is pinned to 2**, and the notice says so rather than saying "or
later". Under §8.3 an unversioned variant reference lets a licensee pick any
version CERN publishes, which is a term this project would not have read.
Adopting a later version is a later decision, taken against its own text.
